        <span class="card__phase-label">Phase 05</span>
        <h2 class="card__heading">Harness Engineering</h2>
        <p class="card__body">A raw model is not an agent — it becomes one when a harness provides instructions and rule files, tools, sandboxes, orchestration logic, deterministic hooks, and observability. The harness, not the underlying model, dominates the behaviour developers experience: most agent failures trace back to a missing tool, a vague rule, or an absent guardrail rather than model capability.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="card card--6">
        <span class="card__phase-label">Phase 06</span>
        <h2 class="card__heading">Conductors and Orchestrators</h2>
        <p class="card__body">Developers now move fluidly between two modes: conductor — real-time, hands-on direction of an AI pair-programmer inside the IDE — and orchestrator — async, high-level delegation to background agents that produce pull requests without a human writing a line of code. The orchestrator role demands specification, decomposition, evaluation, and system-design skills over syntax fluency.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="card card--7">
        <span class="card__phase-label">Phase 07</span>
        <h2 class="card__heading">The Economics of AI Development</h2>
        <p class="card__body">Vibe coding appears cheap upfront but hides compounding operational costs: token burn from unstructured prompting loops, maintenance debt from inconsistent AI-generated code, and expensive security remediation. Agentic engineering requires upfront investment in context, tests, and harness infrastructure but dramatically lowers per-feature marginal cost — especially through intelligent model routing that reserves large frontier models for complex tasks and cheaper models for deterministic work.</p>
