188	  <div class="container">
189	
190	    <!-- ── Header ─────────────────────────────────────────── -->
191	    <header class="header">
192	      <span class="header__eyebrow">High-Level Design Summary</span>
193	      <h1 class="header__title">The New SDLC With Vibe Coding</h1>
194	      <p class="header__subtitle">From ad-hoc prompting to agentic engineering — how AI is reshaping every phase of the software development life cycle, and what it demands from developers, teams, and organisations.</p>
195	      <p class="header__meta">Source: Google — "The New SDLC With Vibe Coding" (Agents Whitepaper Series, May 2026) · Addy Osmani, Shubham Saboo, Sokratis Kartakis</p>
196	    </header>
197	
198	    <!-- ── Phase cards ─────────────────────────────────────── -->
199	    <main class="phases">
200	
201	      <article class="card card--1">
202	        <span class="card__phase-label">Phase 01</span>
203	        <h2 class="card__heading">From Syntax to Intent</h2>
204	        <p class="card__body">Programming is undergoing its most significant transformation since high-level languages: developers now express <em>what</em> they want rather than <em>how</em> to build it, with AI agents handling implementation. By early 2026, 85% of professional developers regularly use AI coding agents and an estimated 41% of all new code is AI-generated.</p>
205	      </article>
206	
207	      <article class="card card--2">
208	        <span class="card__phase-label">Phase 02</span>
209	        <h2 class="card__heading">The Vibe Coding to Agentic Engineering Spectrum</h2>
210	        <p class="card__body">The decisive differentiator is not whether AI is used, but how much structure, verification, and human judgment surrounds its output. Vibe coding — casual prompting with minimal review — suits prototypes and personal projects; agentic engineering — automated test suites, CI/CD gates, and LM-judge evals — is required for production systems that organisations depend on.</p>
211	      </article>
212	
213	      <article class="card card--3">
214	        <span class="card__phase-label">Phase 03</span>
215	        <h2 class="card__heading">Context Engineering</h2>
216	        <p class="card__body">Output quality depends less on clever prompts than on richness of context: instructions, knowledge, memory, examples, tools, and guardrails. The boundary between static context (always loaded) and dynamic context (pulled on demand via Agent Skills) is a first-class architectural decision — too much static context wastes tokens, too little causes agents to forget critical rules.</p>
217	      </article>
218	
219	      <article class="card card--4">
220	        <span class="card__phase-label">Phase 04</span>
221	        <h2 class="card__heading">AI Across Every SDLC Phase</h2>
222	        <p class="card__body">Requirements become a live human-AI conversation producing specs and prototypes simultaneously. Architecture stays human-centric for trade-off decisions. Implementation shifts from writing to reviewing and guiding AI output. Testing demands both output evaluation and trajectory evaluation. Code review and deployment gain AI-powered augmentation, and maintenance of once-impenetrable legacy codebases becomes tractable.</p>
223	      </article>
224	
225	      <article class="card card--5">
226	        <span class="card__phase-label">Phase 05</span>
227	        <h2 class="card__heading">Harness Engineering</h2>
228	        <p class="card__body">A raw model is not an agent — it becomes one when a harness provides instructions and rule files, tools, sandboxes, orchestration logic, deterministic hooks, and observability. The harness, not the underlying model, dominates the behaviour developers experience: most agent failures trace back to a missing tool, a vague rule, or an absent guardrail rather than model capability.</p>
229	      </article>
230	
231	      <article class="card card--6">
232	        <span class="card__phase-label">Phase 06</span>
233	        <h2 class="card__heading">Conductors and Orchestrators</h2>
234	        <p class="card__body">Developers now move fluidly between two modes: conductor — real-time, hands-on direction of an AI pair-programmer inside the IDE — and orchestrator — async, high-level delegation to background agents that produce pull requests without a human writing a line of code. The orchestrator role demands specification, decomposition, evaluation, and system-design skills over syntax fluency.</p>
235	      </article>
236	
237	      <article class="card card--7">
238	        <span class="card__phase-label">Phase 07</span>
239	        <h2 class="card__heading">The Economics of AI Development</h2>
240	        <p class="card__body">Vibe coding appears cheap upfront but hides compounding operational costs: token burn from unstructured prompting loops, maintenance debt from inconsistent AI-generated code, and expensive security remediation. Agentic engineering requires upfront investment in context, tests, and harness infrastructure but dramatically lowers per-feature marginal cost — especially through intelligent model routing that reserves large frontier models for complex tasks and cheaper models for deterministic work.</p>
241	      </article>
